routes.brief:
This **joins the Part II proposed by PAPER-FENG-YUN-ZHANG-24**, whose brief already says to plan it together with “the Yun–Zhang PGL_2 theory it generalizes, whose Octahedron Lemma and Hecke-action computation it imports”. Yun–Zhang, Ann. of Math. 186 (2017), 767–911, is that theory and the first higher-derivative formula of the programme: for an everywhere unramified cuspidal π of PGL_2 over a function field, an étale double cover X′ → X and any even r, the r-th derivative of the normalized base-change L-function equals the self-intersection of the Heegner–Drinfeld cycle in the middle-dimensional Chow group of the moduli of PGL_2-shtukas with r legs (Theorems 1.2 and 1.6). r = 0 recovers Waldspurger's formula and r = 1 the Gross–Zagier formula over function fields.

Import, never re-plan:
- GS.0–GS.2 for Bun_G, the Hecke stacks and the multi-leg shtuka stacks Sht^μ_n, Sht^r_G with their Deligne–Mumford property and smoothness (Theorem 5.4 and Corollary 5.7 are the GL_n/PGL_2 case);
- SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.5 for Chow groups of stacks, refined Gysin maps and intersection products, **including the two general results of Appendix A** (Proposition A.5 on refined Gysin maps versus Riemann–Roch, and the Octahedron Lemma A.10), which this same extraction routes there as a source: they are statements about intersection theory on Deligne–Mumford stacks, not about shtukas, and the Part II should import them rather than restate them;
- EtaleDualityAndPerverseSheaves EDC.5 and EDC.7 for the perverse t-structure, middle extension, smallness and the decomposition theorem; EDC.8 for cohomological correspondences and trace classes, together with the Frobenius-graph trace formula of §A.4 (Lemma A.11 and Proposition A.12), also routed as a source of EDC.8 here;
- FunctionFieldArithmetic FA.6 for everywhere unramified automorphic forms, the spherical Hecke algebra and the Satake isomorphism, and AutomorphicLFunctionsAndLocalFactors AL.2 with FA.5 for the standard and adjoint L-functions and the Riemann hypothesis over function fields;
- GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights GZ.5 for Waldspurger's period formula, whose relative-trace-formula proof in the everywhere unramified function-field case (§§2 and 4 here) is routed there as a source; this Part II needs only its conclusion, Theorem 4.7.

Construct, in the order the proof uses them:
- the geometrization of the analytic orbital integrals: the moduli N_d with its local systems L_d and the identity J(γ, h_D, s) = Σ_d q^{(2d_{12}−d)s}Tr(Frob_a, (Rf_{N_d,*}L_d)_a) (§3);
- the Hecke self-correspondences Sht^r_G(h_D) as cycle classes on Sht^r_G × Sht^r_G, the ring homomorphism H → cCh_{2r} and the self-adjointness of the action (§5.3), the torus shtukas Sht^μ_T, the Heegner–Drinfeld cycle θ^μ_*[Sht^μ_T] and I_r(f) with its independence of μ (§§5.4–5.5);
- the Hitchin-type moduli M_d with f_M : M_d → A_d, the identification of I_r(h_D) with a trace of (f_{M,!}[H♦])^r ∘ Frob and with a local intersection number against the graph of Frobenius (Theorems 6.5–6.6), and the auxiliary stacks and dimension estimates that make the refined Gysin maps defined (§§6.3–6.4, including Lemma 5.9);
- the cohomological spectral decomposition: the H-action on H^*_c(Sht_G), the stratification by instability with the cohomological constant term and the Satake intertwining (§§7.2–7.3), the finiteness theorems making H_ℓ a finitely generated algebra of Krull dimension one, and the resulting orthogonal decomposition of V and V′ (Theorems 7.14 and 7.16);
- the comparison: Rf_{M,*}Q_ℓ ≅ ⊕_{i,j}K_i ⊠ K_j with f_{M,!}[H♦] acting by (d − 2j), Rf_{N_d,*}L_d ≅ (K_{d_{11}} ⊠ K_{d_{12}}) as a middle extension, and hence Theorem 8.1 for h_D of large degree, extended to all f ∈ H by the density Lemma 9.1 (Theorem 9.2).

Keep the hypotheses visible: p > 2; X′ → X étale with X′ geometrically connected (so T is nonsplit and F′/F unramified everywhere); π everywhere unramified; r even for Theorems 1.2 and 1.8 (Remark 1.9 explains what odd r would need); and E_π totally real in Theorem 1.7.

Tests:
- r = 0 must reproduce Waldspurger's formula (Remark 1.3) and r = 1 the Gross–Zagier formula;
- f = 1_K must give the Kronecker limit formula 2^{r+2}(log q)^{−r}L^{(r)}(η, 0) (Corollary 2.5 and Theorem 1.8), and 4L(η,0) + q − 2 for r = 0 (Remark 9.3);
- I_r(f) must be independent of μ (Lemma 5.16), and both sides must vanish for odd r;
- the intersection pairing must be positive definite on the cuspidal part (Theorem 1.7), which consumes the super-positivity of Appendix B.

Then plan the sequel, Yun–Zhang II (Ann. of Math. 189 (2019), 393–526), which removes the unramifiedness restriction — it is a prerequisite of this file, not of the Feng–Yun–Zhang part.
